/*
 * A `_restrict` action atom whose TIME POINT is free.
 *
 * `rewrite` (Theory/Model/Restriction.hs:90-128) abstracts every subterm of
 * every atom that carries only free variables, and it reaches the terms of an
 * atom through `traverse` on `ProtoAtom`, which is
 * `Action <$> f i <*> traverse f fa` (Theory/Model/Atom.hs:139-140) — the time
 * point BEFORE the fact's arguments.  `Foldable` folds them in the same order
 * (Theory/Model/Atom.hs:130-131), so `freesList` of the rewritten formula, and
 * with it the argument list of the generated `Restr_A_1` fact, carries the
 * time point's fresh variable first.
 *
 * Here `#i` is free, so it is abstracted into the message-sorted `x` and
 * `f(x, y)` into `x.1`: the restriction reads
 * `(Restr_A_1( x, x.1 ) @ #NOW) ⇒ (B( x.1 ) @ x)` and the rule's appended
 * action reads `Restr_A_1( #i, f(x, y) )`.
 *
 * No corpus theory reaches this: every `_restrict` action atom in the examples
 * tree binds its time point, so no fresh variable is ever minted for one and
 * the two orders coincide.
 *
 * Mode `match`: the two engines agree on this shape.
 *
 * Compared slices: the wellformedness report, which names the free `#i` as an
 * unbound variable of the rule, and the `theory … end` echo, which carries
 * both the generated restriction and the rewritten rule (see check.sh).
 */
theory S6RestrictActionTimepoint
begin

functions: f/2

rule A:
  [ Fr( ~k ), In( x ), In( y ) ]
  --[ _restrict( B( f(x, y) ) @ #i ), S( ~k ) ]->
  [ Out( ~k ) ]

end
